# shape-pattern-violations.rq (swarm audit wnl2yhbgm finding #30)
# Inputs: ONE oxigraph store holding a shapes graph (SHACL NodeShapes with
# sh:targetClass + sh:property [ sh:path ... ; sh:pattern "..." ]) and instance
# data. Shape-driven, same discipline as registry-missing-fields.rq and
# shape-closed-violations.rq: the regex to enforce is read from the shape
# graph's own sh:pattern declarations, never duplicated in Rust or here.
# Outputs: rows (?entry ?field) — one per (targeted node, property value that
# fails its shape's sh:pattern) pair. A non-empty result means the instance
# violates a declared pattern constraint; the caller maps rows to its typed
# refusal, same as the other two shape queries.
# Determinism: ORDER BY ?entry ?field over a fixed graph.
#
# Before this query existed, `shape_violations` (dispatch.rs) only ran
# registry-missing-fields.rq and shape-closed-violations.rq -- neither
# interprets sh:pattern at all, so every sh:pattern declaration already
# present in this crate's shapes (dispatch-shapes.ttl's disp:dispatchId,
# arazzo-shapes.ttl's several identifier fields) was silently inert: SHACL
# triples sitting in the shapes graph that no query ever consulted.
PREFIX sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>
PREFIX rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>

SELECT ?entry ?field
WHERE {
  ?shape sh:targetClass ?class ;
         sh:property ?ps .
  ?ps sh:path ?field ;
      sh:pattern ?pattern .
  ?entry a ?class ;
         ?field ?value .
  FILTER(!REGEX(STR(?value), ?pattern))
}
ORDER BY ?entry ?field
